  "problems": [
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "number": 1,
      "title": "Problem 1",
      "sourcePages": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "You have a bag with some black balls and some white balls. You put your hand into the bag and pull out a pair of balls."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "(a) If both balls are black, you throw away one and return one to the bag."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "(b) If both balls are white, you throw them both away."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "(c) If the balls are of different colours, you throw away the black ball and return the white ball to the bag."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "You can keep doing this so long as there are at least two balls left in the bag. The process stops when the bag is empty or has only a single ball left."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "For instance, suppose we start with 2 black balls and 2 white balls. The various ways the game can evolve are described below."
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "src": "figures/zio2012-p1-tree.png",
          "alt": "Game tree starting from {2 black, 2 white}. Pick 1 black, 1 white leads to {1 black, 2 white}; Pick 2 black leads to {2 black, 0 white}... shown with arrows: from {2 black, 2 white}: pick 1 black 1 white -> {1 black, 2 white}; pick 2 black -> {1 black, 2 white}; pick 2 white -> {2 black, 0 white}. From {1 black, 2 white}: pick 1 black 1 white -> {0 black, 2 white}; pick 2 white -> {1 black, 0 white}. From {2 black, 0 white}: pick 2 black -> {1 black, 0 white}. From {0 black, 2 white}: pick 2 white -> {0 black, 0 white}.",
          "caption": null,
          "source": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Suppose you start with a given number of black and white balls, play the game and you end up with a single ball in the bag. Let $N$ denote the number of times you pulled out pairs of balls. We are interested in answering the following questions."
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "What are the minimum and maximum values for $N$ for which you end with a single ball in the bag?",
            "Is the last ball remaining in the bag always white, always black or of either colour?"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "In the example worked out above, if we end up with a single ball in the bag, it must be black. Also, the minimum and maximum values of $N$ are both 2. Note that we are not interested in the value of $N$ in situations where the bag becomes empty."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Compute the minimum and maximum values of $N$ as well as the possibilities for the colour of the last ball for each of the following initial contents of the bag."
        }
      ],
