    {
      "id": "p4",
      "number": 4,
      "title": "Problem 4",
      "sourcePages": [
        4,
        5,
        6
      ],
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A shop gets its supplies in cartons, each with a unique serial number. These cartons are stored in a cupboard with many shelves. The shelves are arranged from top to bottom. In each shelf, cartons are arranged from left to right."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The shop assistant uses a strange system to store cartons on the shelves. When a carton numbered $K$ arrives in the shop, he places it according to the following rules."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "(a) If $K$ is bigger than the serial numbers of all the cartons on the top shelf, he adds $K$ at the right end of the top shelf."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "(b) Otherwise, he finds the carton with the smallest serial number bigger than $K$ in the top shelf. Suppose this carton has number $L$. He replaces carton $L$ by carton $K$ and inserts carton $L$ into the second shelf using the same pair of rules."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "In other words, if $L$ is bigger than all the serial numbers in the second shelf, carton $L$ is added at the right end of the second shelf."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Otherwise, it displaces carton $M$, where $M$ is the smallest serial number on the second shelf that is bigger than $L$. Carton $M$ is then inserted into the third self using the same pair of rules, and so on."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "There are always enough empty shelves with adequate free space on each shelf to complete this process."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Separately, he records in his notebook the order in which positions get filled in the shelves."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Here is an example to illustrate the method. Suppose six cartons numbered 32, 43, 21, 65, 54 and 11 arrive in that order. Then, the arrangement of the cartons on the shelves and the record in the notebook after each item arrives is as follows."
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "src": "figures/zio2012-p4-notebook.png",
          "alt": "Table of events (32 arrives, 43 arrives, 21 arrives, 65 arrives, 54 arrives, 11 arrives) with the shelf contents and notebook entries after each event. After the final event the shelves read: 11 43 54 / 21 65 / 32 and the notebook reads: 1 2 4 / 3 5 / 6.",
          "caption": null,
          "source": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Remember that the notebook records the order in which the positions on the shelves are occupied. Thus, the entry 5 in the final notebook after all six items have arrived indicates that the second position in the second shelf became occupied when the fifth carton arrived, even though the actual carton in that position, 65, was the fourth carton to arrive, not the fifth."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The manager of the shop has a problem. From the arrangement of cartons on the shelves and the record in the notebook, he must figure out the order in which the cartons originally arrived, so that old stocks don’t accumulate. For instance, from the final pair of entries in the table above, he should recover the fact that the original sequence of cartons was 32, 43, 21, 65, 54, 11."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Each case below describes the contents of the shelves and the corresponding entries in the notebook after a sequence of cartons has arrived. You have to calculate and write down the original sequence in which the cartons arrived."
        }
      ],
